      "evidence": "The deck is readable as a share deck — each slide's purpose is clear from its headline. However, several slides raise questions a reader without verbal context cannot answer: Slide 3 describes a CLI but does not explain what the AI does or why this is different from a shell script wrapper; Slide 6 lists three raise scenarios with runway months but no milestones, leaving a reader unsure what the capital is for. DocSend notes that investors spend roughly 2:30 of real attention on a share deck; these gaps consume that time without resolving the core questions.",
      "notes": "Add a one-sentence context line to Slide 3 explaining what the CLI does differently from manual integration, and add milestone targets to Slide 6 so the ask is self-explanatory without verbal accompaniment."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 states 'Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration,' quantifying the time cost and naming the ICP. However, Sequoia's problem-slide framework requires the user/buyer distinction (who pays?), the moment of pain, and a quantified cost of inaction in dollars or missed revenue — not just time. The slide does not identify who writes the check, does not translate the time cost into dollars, and includes no customer evidence (discovery interviews, quotes, or pilot commitments). YC explicitly requires discovery evidence on the problem slide.",
      "notes": "Add three elements: (1) buyer identification — 'Paid directly by the founder, no procurement cycle'; (2) dollar cost — 'At a $150/hr developer rate, 20+ hours/week = $3,000+/week per founder'; (3) discovery evidence — 'Validated across 30+ founder interviews; 8 agreed to pilot.' This transforms the slide from a symptom description into a business case."
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 states 'Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI.' This is a feature description — it names what the product does technically but shows no before/after workflow, no outcome for the user, and no product evidence (screenshot, demo, or code example). Sequoia's solution-slide standard requires showing the workflow change (what the user did before, what they do now, and what result they get). DocSend notes that the era of Minimum Viable PowerPoint is over — even seed decks need product evidence. The 'AI-native' label from Slide 1 also has no payoff here.",
      "notes": "Replace the single sentence with a 3-step flow: (1) Before: developer switches between 4 tools, 20+ hours/week; (2) With Acmecorp: one CLI command; (3) Result: a specific outcome the user experiences (time saved, errors eliminated, faster shipping). Add a code snippet or screenshot as the primary visual, and name what the AI specifically does that a non-AI CLI could not."
    },
    {
      "id": "why_now_has_catalyst",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no 'Why Now' slide. No macro catalyst — regulatory shift, new platform primitive, behavioral change, cost curve collapse — is identified anywhere in the six slides. Sequoia asks 'Why wasn't this built before?' as a required question at seed. The missing slides section of the slide review flags this as an important gap.",
      "notes": "Add a Why Now slide explaining what macro shift makes this the right moment: for example, the proliferation of AI-native SaaS tools requiring more integrations, the growth of the indie hacker ecosystem, or a new platform primitive (GitHub Copilot APIs, Stripe's new billing model). A strong why-now argument makes the timing feel inevitable rather than coincidental."
    },
    {
      "id": "market_bottom_up",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no market sizing slide. No ICP count, no willingness-to-pay figure, and no bottom-up TAM calculation appears anywhere. The slide review's missing slides section flags this as a critical gap. Per Sequoia, bottom-up sizing — ICP count × willingness to pay × adoption rate — is required at seed; without it, investors cannot evaluate whether $8K MRR is the start of something large or represents most of the available market.",
      "notes": "Add a bottom-up market slide: estimate the number of indie SaaS founders globally (e.g., from Indie Hackers, Product Hunt, or similar community data), multiply by a willingness-to-pay figure anchored to the current pricing, and apply a realistic adoption rate. Show an expandability layer as a second step."
    },
    {
      "id": "competition_honest",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no competition slide. No direct competitors, no incumbent alternatives (Zapier, n8n, custom scripts), and no 'do nothing' option are addressed anywhere in the six slides. DocSend data shows investors spend 88% more time on competition slides in successful decks than unsuccessful ones; the complete absence reads as avoidance rather than confidence. The slide review's missing slides section flags this as a critical gap.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide mapping: direct competitors (other CLI tools), incumbents (Zapier, n8n, manual scripts), and the 'do nothing / build in-house' default. Use 2–3 concrete differentiators — not a generic 2×2 matrix — to explain why Acmecorp wins on each."
    },
    {
      "id": "business_model_clear",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no business model slide. No pricing model, no price level, no gross margin structure, and no buyer-vs-user distinction are stated anywhere. For an 'AI-native' product, investors specifically look for compute economics and a path to 60%+ gross margins. The slide review's missing slides section flags this as a critical gap.",
      "notes": "Add a business model slide covering: pricing model (seat-based, usage-based, or outcome-based), a price point or range, who pays vs. who uses the product, and a gross margin target. For an AI product, add a one-line note on compute costs and the path to margin improvement (caching, model optimization, etc.)."
    },
    {
      "id": "gtm_has_proof",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no GTM slide. Slide 4 mentions 24 paying customers and 18% MoM growth but does not identify the acquisition channel, sales cycle, or early conversion data. Per Sequoia and DocSend, a GTM slide must define ICP, channel (PLG, sales-led, partner-led), and provide early channel proof. The slide review's missing slides section flags this as an important gap. Twenty-four customers is proof of demand but not proof of a repeatable channel.",
      "notes": "Add a GTM slide showing the primary acquisition channel (e.g., PLG via CLI install, Hacker News, Product Hunt), how the sales cycle works, and what early channel proof exists (e.g., 'All 24 customers acquired organically from two HN posts, zero paid spend — $0 CAC to date')."
    },
    {
      "id": "team_has_depth",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 5 names two co-founders as 'ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product),' demonstrating complementary functional skills. The prior employers are high-signal for a developer-tools company. However, no accomplishments are listed — employer names without achievements do not convey what either founder built or led. No founder-market fit statement is included. No advisory board is mentioned. Since the deck was described in text rather than uploaded as a file, the absence of photos and LinkedIn links is not scored per the criterion's own scope. DocSend data shows investors spend roughly 30% of their total deck attention on the team slide.",
      "notes": "Add one concrete achievement per founder (e.g., 'Led Stripe's payments API, processing $X billion in volume') and a one-sentence founder-market fit statement (e.g., 'Both founders built indie SaaS products on nights and weekends before starting Acmecorp — they are the customer'). Per YC, explaining why these two founders are uniquely positioned to win is the most compelling thing a seed team slide can include."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_structure",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The six slides present (Title, Problem, Solution, Traction, Team, Ask) appear in the correct order relative to the seed expected_framework (Company Purpose → Problem → Solution → Traction KPIs → Team → Ask/Milestones). No slide is out of sequence. Missing slides are scored by the criteria that own them, not here."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_traction",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 4 shows '$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months),' addressing the seed requirement for MRR and growth rate. However, the seed framework also requires retention data (churn, NRR) and unit economics context (CAC, acquisition channel), neither of which is shown. At $8K MRR (~$96K ARR), the traction is also below the Carta/SaaStr seed benchmark of $300K–$500K ARR, which the deck does not address or contextualize. The 18% MoM growth rate is strong and implies reaching the ARR benchmark in roughly 10 months, but this trajectory is not shown.",
      "notes": "Add one retention metric (even 'X% of customers active after 3 months'), one channel attribution line, and a projected MRR trajectory showing the implied ARR at current growth rate. This contextualizes the below-benchmark current traction against a credible near-term milestone."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_financials",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no financials slide. Slide 6 (Ask) shows three raise scenarios with associated runway months but no operating model, revenue assumptions, gross margin trajectory, or burn breakdown. At seed, a16z and Carta expect an 18–24-month operating model showing how capital converts to milestones. The slide review's missing slides section flags this as an important gap.",
      "notes": "Add a simple operating model slide: current burn rate, revenue growth assumptions, gross margin target, and runway by scenario. This is distinct from the Ask slide — it shows the team's financial thinking and gives investors a basis for evaluating the plan, not just the headline number."
    },
    {
