      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 6 presents three explicit raise scenarios — '$1.0M (14 months of runway), $1.5M (20 months), or $2.0M (26 months)' — which constitutes a clear ask rather than a missing one. However, no milestones are tied to any scenario, no use-of-funds breakdown is provided, and no next-round readiness framing is given. The 2026 standard requires dollars → milestones → next-round readiness (or default-alive path).",
      "notes": "Add milestone targets to each scenario: e.g., '$1.5M: 20 months runway, reach $500K ARR, hire 2 engineers, position for Series A.' Include a three-line use-of-funds split (e.g., 60% engineering, 25% GTM, 15% infrastructure). Address the 14-month scenario: Carta data makes 18 months a hard expectation — presenting 14 months invites the wrong conversation."
    },
    {
      "id": "round_size_realistic",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The ask ranges from $1.0M to $2.0M. The seed benchmark per PitchBook/NVCA and Carta 2025 data is $2M–$6M (median ~$3.8M per the stage profile). The $1.0M lower scenario falls below the $2M seed floor, and $2.0M sits at the bottom of the typical seed range. The ask is not dramatically out of range, but the spread across scenarios and the below-median anchor may signal a bridge or pre-seed raise to seed investors rather than a core seed round.",
      "notes": "Consider narrowing the ask to a single amount in the $1.5M–$2.5M range with a clear milestone case, rather than three scenarios that span sub-seed to seed. A single, defended ask reads as more operationally deliberate than a range."
