  "overall_narrative_assessment": "The deck has a strong foundation: a specific ICP, a quantified problem, real paying customers, and meaningful month-over-month growth. The five slides that exist tell a coherent story from problem to ask. But at six slides for a seed round, the deck covers only half the expected framework \u2014 market sizing, competition, business model, go-to-market, and financials are all absent. An investor finishing this deck knows what Acmecorp does and that it has early traction, but cannot answer three questions that determine whether to take a meeting: how large is the opportunity, who else is doing this and why does Acmecorp win, and how does the business make money. The 'AI-native' positioning introduced on Slide 1 is never substantiated \u2014 the solution is described as a CLI wrapper, which is a credible product but not obviously AI. At seed, the narrative arc should run problem \u2192 solution \u2192 why now \u2192 market \u2192 traction \u2192 business model \u2192 competition \u2192 GTM \u2192 team \u2192 financials \u2192 ask; the current deck runs problem \u2192 solution \u2192 traction \u2192 team \u2192 ask. The missing slides are not minor omissions: competition and business model are the two sections investors probe hardest at seed, and their absence will stop most first meetings before they start.",
